# Ülke paritesi — one change, six countries, applied (no exceptions)

**Read and follow** `.cursor/skills/ulke-paritesi-duzeltme/SKILL.md` before closing any
core/shared change, revision, or new feature that is not Turkey-only by law.

Boss rule (Kaan, 2026-10-16, hardened 2026-10-18 — verbatim): "Also is the same rule for
all the 6 countries going forward. Any change in turkiye must be applied to all 6 country
from now on based on that specific countries, language, rules, regulations, government and
medical institutions directives and rules. no exceptions here any longer as well."

**Hardening, 2026-10-18.** Scope widens from "bug fix or UX fix" to **any core/shared
change, revision, or new feature that is not Turkey-only by law** (mirrors
`cross-specialty-parity`'s own scope language; the exemption list below is unchanged).
Strength widens from "checked" to **"applied"**: checking the six countries and logging a
gap is no longer sufficient on its own for in-scope work — the country-adapted equivalent
must actually be **built and shipped**, each adapted to that country's own language,
laws/regulations, and government/medical-institution directives — never a translated copy
of the Turkish version. This is the same standard already proven on the AU Tier-1
clinical-tools work: mechanical ports of non-Turkey-specific content travel directly;
content with Turkey-specific regulatory/legal substance needs real per-country research
(see `international-data-model-dogrulama` for how identity/report fields adapt).

**Two-axis propagation — operates together, not as alternatives.** A core/shared Türkiye
change must reach **both** (a) all 30 medical branches + 10 Özel Branşlar (40 total, per
`cross-specialty-parity`) **and** (b) all six countries, country-adapted (per this rule) —
in the same change set wherever feasible. When not feasible in the same change set, it is
logged as an explicit, dated follow-up in `docs/OPEN-COMMITMENTS.md` with a named owner
(Kaan vs Claude) — never a silent deferral.

**Branş-specific changes (clarified 2026-10-18, Kaan, verbatim intent): "going forward if
we change something in a specific branch in turkiye then the other 6 countries specific
branc gets updated the same... This way once each country is caught up to the golden
referrence of turkey they will then stary in sync."** The "applied, no exceptions" bar is
not limited to core/shared changes — a **branş-specific** fix or feature made to one
branş in Türkiye carries the same obligation to that same branş in each of the six
countries, country-adapted, in the **same change set, no deferral**.

**No depth gate — build the scaffold, don't wait for it (correction, 2026-10-18, Kaan,
verbatim): "Even if the 6 countries do not match the golden standard of turkiye, you still
go ahead and add the new features to the 6 countries as we go. If the underlying infra is
not there then you build that only so that you can still update the change across the
board to all 6."** A country not yet having that branş's infrastructure is **not** a reason
to defer. When a Türkiye branş-specific change needs to land in a country that doesn't yet
have that branş's infrastructure, build the **minimal real scaffold** needed to host that
specific change now — following the existing `.ulke`-tree/kit pattern already proven for
AU's Tier-1 clinical tools (`NOTYA-ULKE-ARACLAR-AU-07`): a real, working addition, never a
stub that fakes it — so the change ships across all 6 countries in the same cycle.

**Scope boundary (non-negotiable):** "minimal scaffold" means just enough to carry **this
specific change** — not backfilling that branş's full depth to the Turkish golden-reference
level in one shot. Full-depth closeout for a branş in a country is its **own larger,
separately-sequenced job** (the same AU-first pattern already running, per
`NOTYA-ULKE-ARACLAR-AU-07`). Minimal-scaffold-as-you-go and full-depth-closeout are two
different, complementary efforts — never collapse one into the other, and never use "we'll
do the real depth work later" as a reason to skip shipping this change now. End state, in
Kaan's own framing: once a country catches up to the Turkish golden reference for a branş,
this rule is what keeps the two in sync afterward, preventing re-drift.

1. Per country: shared code / forked copy / not applicable / exempt (Turkey-only: MBYS, SGK/SUT/Medula, e-Nabız, e-Reçete, NVI, e-Fatura/GİB).
2. Localized strings per country — never Turkish strings in a country build; Turkish stays untouched unless the code is genuinely shared.
3. Each country's own data model (`international-data-model-dogrulama`) — never Turkish field keys/validators as placeholders.
4. Tests with non-Turkish keys/copy; unported gaps go in `docs/OPEN-COMMITMENTS.md` with date and owner.
5. The commit/PR report must contain one status line per country, each stating **APPLIED**
   (built + shipped), **N/A**, **EXEMPT**, or **OPEN GAP** (logged with date + owner) —
   "checked only" is no longer a valid status on its own for in-scope work.
